  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery highlights a potent 500 mb shortwave trough\ndigging equatorward from Ontario into the Great Lakes basin. As\nthis vorticity maximum phases with the broader cyclonic flow, a\nslow-moving surface low will pivot its wrap-around moisture \nplume and deformation axis back southwestward across central and\neastern Illinois. This setup will maintain overcast skies and \ngenerate scattered to periodic light rain showers, mostly near \nand north of the I-74 corridor.\n\nA sharpening baroclinic zone and its accompanying cold front \nwill advance southward through the state tonight. Strong cold \nair advection in its wake will usher in a significantly cooler, \ndrier continental polar (cP) air mass to finish the workweek.\n\nDeep-layer northwesterly flow following the departing trough \nwill rapidly scour out remaining low-level moisture by Friday \nmorning. An expansive 1024 mb surface high centered over the \nCanadian Prairies will build southeastward into the Midwest for \nthe weekend, establishing a prolonged stretch of tranquil autumn\nweather.\n\nThermal profiles will feel chilly early Friday, with 850 mb \ntemperatures dropping into the -5C to -2C range. Sensible \nweather over the weekend will feature crisp daytime highs in the\nlow-to-mid 50s under mostly sunny skies. Efficient nighttime \nradiational cooling--driven by light winds and a clear, dry \nboundary layer--will foster widespread frost, as overnight lows\nfall into the lower 30s across the region. Agricultural and \nlate-season gardening interests should prepare for widespread \nfrost/freeze potential Friday night and Saturday night.\n\nLooking ahead to next week, ensemble consensus (GEFS/EPS) shows\nstrong agreement on an amplified synoptic pattern anchored by \npersistent mid/upper-level ridging over the central CONUS, \nflanked by an anomalous cut-off low meandering over the Deep \nSouth and Gulf Coast. This blocking regime will limit poleward \nGulf moisture transport, securing dry and quiet weather through \nmid-week. As the air mass modifies and 500 mb heights rise, \ntemperatures will gradually moderate back into the 60s starting \nMonday.",
